# 0011. The room in Neon is measured before it is spent

Status: accepted, 16 November 2026

## Context

Phase 3 adds an advisory corpus with its vectors, a store of triage runs and the checkpoints of the
graph, and the API serves all of it from the same Neon database as everything else. The plan limit
is {{ room.plan_limit if room else "1 GB a project" }}, and this build keeps a ceiling of
{{ data("config/budgets.yaml").neon.storage_ceiling_mb }} MB (config/budgets.yaml). The sync refuses a chunk that would pass the ceiling and writes nothing, so a
full database keeps the site up on old data while nothing new arrives.
{% if room and has("room.neon.size_mb") %}

When the API read the database at {{ room.neon_now.read_at }} it held {{ v("room.neon.size_mb") }} MB, {{ v("room.neon.share") }} of the
ceiling: the event table {{ v("room.neon.cve_event_mb") }} MB, the CVE table {{ v("room.neon.cve_mb") }} MB and the predictions
{{ v("room.neon.prediction_mb") }} MB, almost all of it.
{% endif %}

## What was measured

Only the API reaches Neon, so nothing was tried there. scripts/measure_room.py measures on a Postgres
with pgvector that is not Neon (results/agent/room.json){% if room %}: Postgres {{ room.replica.server_version.split(" ")[0] }} with pgvector {{ room.replica.pgvector }}, the
pgvector Neon offers. Neon runs Postgres {{ room.neon_now.server_version.split(" ")[0] }}; the prediction table, which no sync has updated, takes the
same room in both{% endif %}.
{% if has("room.replica.cve_mb") %}

- **The serving tables, fresh.** The newest serving bundle loaded into an empty schema by the sync's
  own loader, with the event key this decision gives it: the CVE table takes {{ v("room.replica.cve_mb") }} MB against
  {{ v("room.neon.cve_mb") }} MB in Neon, so its updates leave little behind; the predictions take the same {{ v("room.replica.prediction_mb") }} MB. The
  event table, {{ v("room.replica.event_rows") }} rows, takes {{ v("room.replica.cve_event_mb") }} MB, of which {{ v("room.replica.cve_event_index_mb") }} MB are its two indexes.
- **A day of the lake.** Two daily bundles compared row by row with the sync's own digests,
  {{ v("room.churn.days") }} days apart: {{ v("room.churn.cve_new") }} new CVEs, {{ v("room.churn.cve_changed") }} changed CVE rows and {{ v("room.churn.events_new") }} new events, so the
  new rows add {{ v("room.growth_mb_per_day") }} MB a day and the changes leave dead rows behind for autovacuum.
- **The corpus.** {{ v("room.sample.chunks") }} chunks cut from the GitHub advisories that name a CVE the six stand in
  products carry, from the KEV catalog and from the CWE catalog, stored the way step 4 will store
  them, with a full text index, a halfvec(384) column and an HNSW index: {{ v("room.sample.bytes_per_chunk") }} bytes a chunk with its
  indexes. The vectors are random unit vectors from a fixed seed, since the size of a halfvec column
  and of an HNSW graph depends on how many vectors there are, not on their values.
- **The checkpoints.** A fixture graph with the node names of the triage graph and the planned state,
  run to its approval interrupt with the Postgres checkpointer: {{ v("room.checkpoint_kb_waiting") }} KB a run while it waits for a
